}

{
  "scripts": [
    {
      "title": "Stop selling features, start selling outcomes",
      "hook": "Most AI consultants are broke because they sell features. You are not a developer. You are an outcome partner.",
      "script": "Most AI consultants are broke because they sell features. You are not a developer. You are an outcome partner. Here is the thing. A founder does not care about your Claude Code setup or your prompt library. They care about one of three things. Getting more customers. Making those customers worth more. Or cutting costs. I saw a project fail because the dev sold an automation bot, not a profit increase. Stop selling tools. Sell the math. If you can show a business owner how to save 50,000 rupees a month in operational overhead, they will sign your retainer immediately. Build. Automate. Scale.",
      "on_screen_text": "Stop selling features | Sell outcomes instead | The 3 buckets of value | Cut costs | Increase revenue | Build. Automate. Scale.",
      "cta": "Comment 'OUTCOME' and I will send you my scoping template.",
      "source_timestamp": "00:36:00"
    },
    {
      "title": "The niche myth",
      "hook": "Everyone tells you to pick a niche on day one. They are wrong.",
      "script": "Everyone tells you to pick a niche on day one. They are wrong. If you pick a niche before you have done the work, you are just guessing. I suggest you stay broad for your first ten projects. You need reps. You need pattern recognition. You want to see how a logistics firm uses AI versus a SaaS startup. Once you have seen ten different business constraints, the winning niche will pick you. Don't waste time on market research. Ship it before Monday. Do five audits for free. Get the data. Then pick your lane.",
      "on_screen_text": "Stop niching down | Optimize for reps | Get 10 projects first | Pattern recognition > Niche | Ship it before Monday",
      "cta": "Follow for more operator grade AI building tips.",
      "source_timestamp": "10:17:00"
    },
    {
      "title": "The four blank scoping rule",
      "hook": "Stop writing code until you have filled out these four blanks.",
      "script": "Stop writing code until you have filled out these four blanks. This is how you avoid the trap where 87 percent of AI projects never reach production. One. The current baseline metric. Two. The specific business constraint. Three. The target outcome. Four. The project deadline. If you cannot fill these in, you do not have a project. You have a hobby. Founders pay for certainty. Using this template, I have turned 10,000 rupee audits into 1 lakh rupee projects. Stop guessing what to build. Start scoping what to solve.",
      "on_screen_text": "Stop coding blindly | The 4-blank template | Baseline | Constraint | Target | Deadline | Founders pay for certainty",
      "cta": "Save this video for your next client meeting.",
      "source_timestamp": "17:44:00"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "newsletter": {
    "subject_line": "Stop selling AI features as a one-person business",
    "preview_text": "Most AI consultants fail because they solve technical problems instead of business ones.",
    "body": "<h3>Builders sell features. Partners sell outcomes.</h3><p>Most freelancers pitching AI services are doing it wrong. They show up with a list of tools or a flashy demo. The client does not care. Your client cares about one thing: moving a number on their P&L.</p><p>I looked at the data. Demand for AI integration skills grew 178 percent year-over-year. Yet, only 13 percent of AI projects actually make it from a proof of concept to production. That gap is where your business lives.</p><p>Stop calling yourself an AI builder. Start acting like a partner who solves business constraints. If you cannot explain how your code helps them get more customers, increase customer value, or cut costs, you are not selling a business solution. You are selling a hobby.</p><h3>Three rules for your first 10 engagements</h3><p>Forget the advice about niching down on day one. You do not have enough data to know what works. Instead, focus on these three steps to build your practice:</p><ul><li><strong>Optimize for reps, not niches.</strong> Take the first five to ten projects you can find regardless of the industry. You need pattern recognition to see which problems recur. Once you see a pattern, then you build your moat.</li><li><strong>Use the four-blank scoping discipline.</strong> Before you write a single line of code, fill in these blanks: We will improve [Metric] from [Baseline] to [Target] by [Date]. If you cannot fill those blanks, you do not have a project. You have a meeting.</li><li><strong>Audit before you build.</strong> Do not pitch a full-scale integration. Offer a paid audit to warm contacts. It lowers the barrier to entry and gives you a case study. AI-enabled freelancers earn about 40 percent more per hour than their peers, but only if they can show results.</li></ul><p>Here is the thing. The barrier to entry is dropping. The leverage you get from being a one-person business is higher than it has ever been. But you should not be selling anything you have not built for yourself. If you are just reselling prompt engineering tricks, you will be replaced by a software update within six months.</p><p>Stop rushing for your first yes. Race to your first ten nos instead. You will learn more from a rejection where a founder tells you why they do not care about your AI tool than you will from a friendly LinkedIn comment.</p><p>Build. Automate. Scale.</p>",
    "cta": "Reply to this email with the one business constraint you plan to solve for your next client.",
    "source_timestamp": "04:22:00"
  }
}

